HTTP Parameter Pollution (HPP) is a technique used to bypass input validation and WAF rules by manipulating HTTP headers. Here's an example of how to use HPP to bypass input validation in PHP:

1. Create a form with a hidden input field.

```html
<form action="process.php" method="post">
  <input type="hidden" name="input" value="123">
  <input type="submit" value="Submit">
</form>
```

2. Use JavaScript to manipulate the X-HTTP-Method-Override header.

```javascript
<script>
document.querySelector('form').addEventListener('submit', function(event) {
  event.preventDefault();
  document.querySelector('input[type="hidden"]').value = 'POST';
  document.querySelector('input[type="hidden"]').name = '_method';
  document.querySelector('input[type="hidden"]').dispatchEvent(new Event('input', { bubbles: true }));
  document.querySelector('form').submit();
});
</script>
```

3. In your PHP script, check for the _method parameter.

```php
if ($_POST['_method'] == 'POST') {
  // Process the POST request
} else {
  // Process the GET request
}
```

4. Use the X-HTTP-Method-Override header to bypass input validation.

```php
if ($_POST['input'] == '123') {
  // Do something
}
```

Now, when the form is submitted, the X-HTTP-Method-Override header will be set to 'POST', allowing the script to bypass input validation and process the POST request.

